THE POSITION AFTER THIS CHUNK — AND A MEASURED REASON NOT TO READ A 2-3 %
SLOPE MOVE OUT OF TWO POSITION RUNS.

date:   2026-09-04 (fourth chunk of 2026-09-03)
host:   mac (Apple M4 Pro, aarch64-apple-darwin, 12 cores). Box QUIET: the
        competing-process count printed 0 before and after every run.
tool:   tools/perf_gate.sh, 25 interleaved paired rounds per cell, one untimed
        warmup inside each sample, no -C target-cpu=native, gradient qp 40,
        preset 8, sizes 64/128/256/512.
tree:   ceedbf3c (main) — this chunk's z2 NEON arm plus the sibling
        workspace's inter commits (eb658f4b, 9c397e36, f98c5558).
data:   benchmarks/perf_2026-09-03-arm9-{still,videokey,inter}.{tsv,raw.tsv,meta}

=================== 1. THE POSITION ========================================

  preset 8, port/C      64      128     256     512    slope ratio
  still               0.791    1.402   2.214   2.376     2.46x
  videokey            1.338    1.850   2.400   2.542     2.60x
  inter               1.592    2.040   2.486   2.767     2.82x

**Every cell of all three arms is ident=Y**, including all four inter cells.
Fits: still port 36.985 ms/MP against C's 15.057; videokey 161.31 against
62.04; inter 210.25 against 74.56.

=================== 2. AND WHY THE COMPARISON TO arm8/arm7 IS NOT A RESULT ==

Read against `benchmarks/perf_2026-09-03-arm8-still.meta` (02:15Z, the tree
BEFORE this chunk) the still slope ratio reads 2.40x -> 2.46x, i.e. the port
apparently got 2 % SLOWER while C stayed flat (15.043 -> 15.057 ms/MP).

**That is session drift, and it is measured, not assumed.** A PAIRED A/B of the
two trees in ONE session, same harness, same box state, 512x512 p8, n=25,
ident=Y:

  base 76fdb802 (arm8's tree)  ->  cand ceedbf3c (this tree)   **1.013x FASTER**
  base 76fdb802 + z2 only      ->  cand ceedbf3c               0.989x

So the current tree is 1.3 % FASTER than arm8's at that cell, not 2 % slower.
The same 76fdb802 binary measured **9.493 ms** in the arm8 session and
**9.797 ms** in this one — a ~3 % port-side offset between sessions, larger
than any single chunk's win.

**THE RULE: a position run's slope ratio cannot resolve a 2-3 % change across
SESSIONS.** Only the paired A/B can, because both binaries meet the same box
state within the same interleaved rounds. Every earlier position record in this
directory already says "read this as position, not attribution" for C's drift;
this one adds that the PORT side drifts too, by about the same amount.

The second A/B line above also separates this chunk from the sibling's: z2 is
worth ~2.4 % at 512 p8 still and the three inter commits landed alongside it
cost ~1.1 % there, for a net 1.3 %. That is a two-binary difference of trees,
not a per-commit attribution of the sibling's work.

=================== NOT ESTABLISHED ========================================
  * WHY the port side drifts ~3 % between sessions on this box. Not measured.
  * Any preset but 8 for the position, size beyond 512, content but gradient.
  * The sibling's -1.1 % at 512 p8 still is a difference of TREES (three
    commits), not an attribution to any one of them.
